    "content": "Dave Jerden brought to Sound of White Noise a production philosophy completely different from anything Anthrax had previously worked with. Where Eddie Kramer had given Among the Living a live, three-dimensional room sound and Mark Dodson had given Persistence of Time a hyper-articulated, almost clinical thrash production, Jerden brought a thick, organic, mid-range-heavy aesthetic informed by his work with Alice in Chains, Jane's Addiction, and Social Distortion.\n\n**Room and Ambience:**\nA&M Studios' tracking room — the same room that had hosted countless classic rock and metal recordings — was central to Jerden's drum sound. He preserved natural room ambience aggressively, blending close mics with room and overhead microphones to create a sense of physical space around the kit. The result is drums that sound like they were played in a real room rather than constructed in isolation.\n\n**Microphone Setup:**\n- Kick drums: Close mics inside each bass drum (Shure Beta 52 or similar) blended with subkick microphones for low-end weight\n- Snare: Top mic (Shure SM57) and bottom mic for combined attack and wire sensitivity\n- Toms: Close mics on each (Sennheiser MD421 standard)\n- Overheads: Pair of large-diaphragm condensers for cymbal wash and stereo image\n- Room mics: Distant pairs capturing the natural ambience of A&M's tracking room\n\n**The Double Bass Sound:**\nJerden tuned the kick drums lower than thrash-era productions, prioritizing thud and weight over clicky attack. The result is a kick sound on tracks like \"Only\" and \"Burst\" that has physical impact — you feel it as much as you hear it. This was a deliberate alignment with the groove-metal aesthetic the album was reaching for.\n\n**Mix Balance:**\nThe snare on Sound of White Noise sits slightly less forward than on the thrash records — embedded more deeply in the overall mix to support the song's mid-tempo groove rather than dominate it. Cymbals are present but controlled, never overwhelming the dense guitar and bass work. Toms are warm and fat, with full mid-range presence that complements Jerden's mid-range-heavy guitar production.",
